WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.557 --> 00:00:01.833
- He's an American rapper,

00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:02.789
record producer,

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:03.719
and entrepreneur.

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:05.634
He rose to fame in 2006

00:00:05.634 --> 00:00:08.009
following the success of his debut album.

00:00:08.009 --> 00:00:08.969
As an entrepreneur,

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:09.919
he's the CEO

00:00:09.919 --> 00:00:12.438
of First and Fifteenth Entertainment.

00:00:12.438 --> 00:00:13.895
He's Lupe Fiasco,

00:00:13.895 --> 00:00:14.728
and here are his

00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:16.895
top ten rules for success.

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:21.186
- Know what you want.

00:00:21.186 --> 00:00:22.677
I always say that about anything.

00:00:22.677 --> 00:00:23.841
It's my number one piece of advice

00:00:23.841 --> 00:00:26.000
to anyone trying to be in any industry,

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.494
is know what you want.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:30.388
Whether it be the most
materialistic thing,

00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:31.693
you want a million dollars,

00:00:31.693 --> 00:00:32.693
and a house,

00:00:33.593 --> 00:00:34.940
or you want to be recognized as

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:35.773
the best of the best.

00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:36.606
Whatever,

00:00:36.606 --> 00:00:37.439
you want ten Grammys,

00:00:37.439 --> 00:00:38.272
whatever.

00:00:38.272 --> 00:00:39.905
Just know what you want,

00:00:39.905 --> 00:00:40.738
you know,

00:00:40.738 --> 00:00:42.637
and then you'll know

00:00:42.637 --> 00:00:43.865
what paths to take

00:00:43.865 --> 00:00:45.592
to get there.

00:00:45.592 --> 00:00:46.425
It's just,

00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:47.258
you know,

00:00:47.258 --> 00:00:48.986
recognizing taking everything serious,

00:00:48.986 --> 00:00:49.819
you know what I'm saying?

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:51.965
Not really putting the age limit,

00:00:51.965 --> 00:00:54.140
or any type of limitation on yourself.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:54.973
You know as opposed to saying,

00:00:54.973 --> 00:00:56.209
"I'm going to do that later",

00:00:56.209 --> 00:00:57.042
it's like,

00:00:57.042 --> 00:00:57.875
why don't you do that now?

00:00:57.875 --> 00:00:58.708
You know?

00:00:58.708 --> 00:00:59.541
And I think of me and my friends

00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:00.374
when we were in high school

00:01:00.374 --> 00:01:02.780
we decided to take music serious

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.099
and put ourselves in a position

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:05.129
where it was like,

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:06.068
do or die.

00:01:06.068 --> 00:01:06.901
You know what I'm saying?

00:01:06.901 --> 00:01:09.295
Like, if we don't do music to the fullest,

00:01:09.295 --> 00:01:10.875
you know, we'll be homeless.

00:01:10.875 --> 00:01:11.903
You know, for real like

00:01:11.903 --> 00:01:12.736
we didn't have no...

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:14.376
We weren't thinking about going to college

00:01:14.376 --> 00:01:15.209
or nothing.

00:01:15.209 --> 00:01:16.593
- You would be homeless for real,

00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:17.785
if it didn't work out in music,

00:01:17.785 --> 00:01:18.618
(expletive beep) would be that

00:01:18.618 --> 00:01:19.620
(expletive beep) up

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:20.482
that you would be out on the streets?

00:01:20.482 --> 00:01:21.401
- We wouldn't have a job.

00:01:21.401 --> 00:01:22.822
We put ourselves in a position

00:01:22.822 --> 00:01:24.433
where we weren't going to go to school.

00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:25.718
You know,

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.329
our future was music.

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:28.162
You know?

00:01:28.162 --> 00:01:28.995
So we didn't have a back-up plan.

00:01:28.995 --> 00:01:30.035
Our back-up plan was like,

00:01:30.035 --> 00:01:31.549
get a government job,

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:32.557
like work for the Post Office

00:01:32.557 --> 00:01:33.390
or somethin' like that.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.223
For real, though.

00:01:34.223 --> 00:01:35.739
I don't know if people believe that

00:01:35.739 --> 00:01:37.774
but my homies will vouch for it

00:01:37.774 --> 00:01:39.608
cause we actually did.

00:01:39.608 --> 00:01:40.894
And that was kind of like the motivation,

00:01:40.894 --> 00:01:41.843
you know,

00:01:41.843 --> 00:01:43.378
the seriousness that we took it.

00:01:43.378 --> 00:01:44.211
It was like,

00:01:44.211 --> 00:01:45.044
"No, we gon' do music,

00:01:45.044 --> 00:01:45.877
we gon' be rappers.

00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:46.710
For real,

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:48.232
this is going to be our future".

00:01:48.232 --> 00:01:50.369
You know I never want to
do the same thing twice.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:51.348
So it's like,

00:01:51.348 --> 00:01:52.926
when you got the superstar,

00:01:52.926 --> 00:01:54.077
or you got the Kick Push,

00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:55.400
or you got the Agaci,

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.364
even in some instances.

00:01:56.364 --> 00:01:57.234
You know,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.140
it was like okay,

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.745
oh the show goes on.

00:01:59.745 --> 00:02:00.578
Okay that's cool,

00:02:00.578 --> 00:02:01.489
now I want to do something else.

00:02:01.489 --> 00:02:02.812
I always want to expand,

00:02:02.812 --> 00:02:04.032
I want my catalog of music

00:02:04.032 --> 00:02:04.869
to be diverse,

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:06.574
so when you look back over time,

00:02:06.574 --> 00:02:08.791
you can get a every Lupe album

00:02:08.791 --> 00:02:10.622
and every song is something different.

00:02:10.622 --> 00:02:11.914
And everything is a different look

00:02:11.914 --> 00:02:13.947
or a different take on something.

00:02:13.947 --> 00:02:15.795
It's not necessarily I just want

00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:16.628
to get on radio,

00:02:16.628 --> 00:02:17.461
cause that's not my thing.

00:02:17.461 --> 00:02:19.323
My thing isn't to be on
the radio all the time.

00:02:19.323 --> 00:02:20.587
I tell the record company that,

00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:21.921
my promo manager will tell you that,

00:02:21.921 --> 00:02:23.254
everybody will tell you it's not,

00:02:23.254 --> 00:02:25.030
you know Lupe is not really,

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.620
he's more with being socially conscious,

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.628
being socially aware,

00:02:28.628 --> 00:02:29.928
speaking things that,

00:02:29.928 --> 00:02:30.781
you know,

00:02:30.781 --> 00:02:32.671
that is barred from the radio.

00:02:32.671 --> 00:02:33.504
That's his thing.

00:02:33.504 --> 00:02:35.621
To get you to play his songs on the radio

00:02:35.621 --> 00:02:36.829
when you know you're not supposed to

00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:37.662
be playing them.

00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:38.495
That's the real fan.

00:02:38.495 --> 00:02:39.336
- [Interviewer] It's saying so much.

00:02:39.336 --> 00:02:40.246
- Exactly.

00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:42.630
You really got to kind of

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:43.843
like just accept it.

00:02:43.843 --> 00:02:45.267
Yeah, you hate me.

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:46.608
Like half the world hates me,

00:02:46.608 --> 00:02:47.441
you know.

00:02:47.441 --> 00:02:48.409
There are people that come up with

00:02:48.409 --> 00:02:51.981
the wildest excuses to
say that I'm ineligible,

00:02:51.981 --> 00:02:52.814
I'm wack,

00:02:52.814 --> 00:02:53.745
or I'm this.

00:02:53.745 --> 00:02:55.266
And it will have nothing
to do with anything.

00:02:55.266 --> 00:02:56.762
I've had people

00:02:56.762 --> 00:02:58.644
they couldn't find anything
wrong with the show,

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:00.953
so they had to go to my shoes.

00:03:00.953 --> 00:03:02.327
You know those people who

00:03:02.327 --> 00:03:04.332
couldn't find anything
wrong with the songs

00:03:04.332 --> 00:03:05.852
so they have to talk about my hair,

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:06.685
you know?

00:03:06.685 --> 00:03:07.518
It's just,

00:03:07.518 --> 00:03:08.442
like...

00:03:08.442 --> 00:03:10.126
And then once you accept
that for what it is,

00:03:10.126 --> 00:03:11.931
that there's a certain amount of people

00:03:11.931 --> 00:03:13.340
that are always gon' be like that

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.528
no matter what you do.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:16.788
And if I look back through my history

00:03:16.788 --> 00:03:17.801
I really had to turn around

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:18.634
and was like

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:19.760
"you've always been the black sheep,

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:20.870
you've always been the outcast,

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:21.703
since high school

00:03:21.703 --> 00:03:22.536
you had dudes that wanted to fight you

00:03:22.536 --> 00:03:24.551
because they didn't like the way

00:03:24.551 --> 00:03:25.547
you wore your glasses,

00:03:25.547 --> 00:03:26.970
they wanted to fight you",

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.613
you know?

00:03:28.613 --> 00:03:29.446
Me and Bishop G,

00:03:29.446 --> 00:03:30.279
my man,

00:03:30.279 --> 00:03:31.282
we first met cause he wanted to fight me.

00:03:31.282 --> 00:03:32.844
(Interviewer laughing)

00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:33.824
Walked into class,

00:03:33.824 --> 00:03:35.311
"Yo, who are you?",

00:03:35.311 --> 00:03:37.417
"Nigga, who are you?".

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:38.250
But it,

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.083
you know,

00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:40.988
it's just when you
embrace it and accept it.

00:03:40.988 --> 00:03:43.290
And then you just send it back at 'em.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.123
You know?

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:44.956
It was like,

00:03:44.956 --> 00:03:45.789
I know you don't like me,

00:03:45.789 --> 00:03:46.794
I don't want to (expletive
beep) with you either.

00:03:46.794 --> 00:03:48.475
I'm a point now where I felt

00:03:48.475 --> 00:03:50.273
everything that I have to do,

00:03:50.273 --> 00:03:51.637
or everything that I'm doing,

00:03:51.637 --> 00:03:53.103
has to have

00:03:53.103 --> 00:03:54.031
you know it has to have

00:03:54.031 --> 00:03:55.179
longevity.

00:03:55.179 --> 00:03:56.618
And it has to be like,

00:03:56.618 --> 00:03:57.451
very real.

00:03:57.451 --> 00:03:58.284
You know?

00:03:58.284 --> 00:03:59.237
It's something that you can't argue.

00:03:59.237 --> 00:04:00.146
We can argue politics,

00:04:00.146 --> 00:04:02.193
we can argue how do we end the violence

00:04:02.193 --> 00:04:03.026
or we how do we do this.

00:04:03.026 --> 00:04:06.009
But you can't argue about fighting cancer.

00:04:06.009 --> 00:04:08.225
And for me it was just a easier space

00:04:08.225 --> 00:04:10.017
to deal with as an artist

00:04:10.017 --> 00:04:10.850
for the backlash.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:11.683
Cause like,

00:04:11.683 --> 00:04:12.516
I dare you to say something about

00:04:12.516 --> 00:04:13.489
this record.

00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:14.916
I dare you to say something about this.

00:04:14.916 --> 00:04:15.842
Um,

00:04:15.842 --> 00:04:16.676
and even if you do it's great

00:04:16.676 --> 00:04:18.295
'cause now we're having a conversation

00:04:18.295 --> 00:04:19.537
about fighting cancer.

00:04:19.537 --> 00:04:20.946
And that's a great thing to do-

00:04:20.946 --> 00:04:22.590
raise awareness and
hopefully raise some money

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.632
at the same time.

00:04:24.632 --> 00:04:26.373
So it's a reaction to kind of that,

00:04:26.373 --> 00:04:27.950
to kind of take myself out of

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.367
being the punching bag for people.

00:04:30.367 --> 00:04:32.257
And then make music that I felt like,

00:04:32.257 --> 00:04:33.595
if I die tomorrow,

00:04:33.595 --> 00:04:34.428
you know,

00:04:34.428 --> 00:04:36.839
I want songs to be like that.

00:04:36.839 --> 00:04:38.342
I want you to remember what I did

00:04:38.342 --> 00:04:39.175
like that.

00:04:39.175 --> 00:04:40.008
You know,

00:04:40.008 --> 00:04:41.199
Lupe put that into the world.

00:04:41.199 --> 00:04:42.032
(rap music)

00:04:42.032 --> 00:04:42.865
One of my, uh,

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:44.330
strengths is story telling

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.147
and concepts.

00:04:46.147 --> 00:04:48.005
As opposed to just jibber-jabber

00:04:48.005 --> 00:04:49.172
and jabbering.

00:04:50.336 --> 00:04:51.882
So when I put together an album,

00:04:51.882 --> 00:04:53.308
I kind of need a framework,

00:04:53.308 --> 00:04:55.057
I need something to go off.

00:04:55.057 --> 00:04:57.594
With 'Lasers' we had a manifesto.

00:04:57.594 --> 00:04:59.776
With 'The Cool' we had this characters

00:04:59.776 --> 00:05:01.543
and these story lines,

00:05:01.543 --> 00:05:03.059
and this zombie who came back to life

00:05:03.059 --> 00:05:04.081
and all these different kind of things

00:05:04.081 --> 00:05:04.914
kind of used him.

00:05:04.914 --> 00:05:05.893
So with this album,

00:05:05.893 --> 00:05:06.726
it was like

00:05:06.726 --> 00:05:08.385
okay, let me take something that's

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:09.371
like,

00:05:09.371 --> 00:05:10.507
that exists,

00:05:10.507 --> 00:05:11.433
America.

00:05:11.433 --> 00:05:12.266
Let's talk about,

00:05:12.266 --> 00:05:13.689
let's use America as a concept.

00:05:13.689 --> 00:05:15.872
And let that be the
character and everything.

00:05:15.872 --> 00:05:17.405
So you know I just stuck to my guns,

00:05:17.405 --> 00:05:19.301
what I'm strong at.

00:05:19.301 --> 00:05:20.625
Me and Bishop G made a pact.

00:05:20.625 --> 00:05:22.055
If we don't make it rapping

00:05:22.055 --> 00:05:23.905
we going to work at the Post Office.

00:05:23.905 --> 00:05:25.071
And it was like,

00:05:25.071 --> 00:05:26.338
we writing raps all day

00:05:26.338 --> 00:05:27.184
12 hours a day

00:05:27.184 --> 00:05:28.017
Go home,

00:05:29.168 --> 00:05:30.093
stay up,

00:05:30.093 --> 00:05:30.982
go to school,

00:05:30.982 --> 00:05:34.766
rap the rap that you
wrote the night before

00:05:34.766 --> 00:05:37.233
and do that for the next 16 weeks

00:05:37.233 --> 00:05:38.329
type situation.

00:05:38.329 --> 00:05:39.679
So,

00:05:39.679 --> 00:05:40.512
you know,

00:05:40.512 --> 00:05:43.321
for me it's about just
the love of the art.

00:05:43.321 --> 00:05:45.229
The love of putting words together.

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:46.062
And like I said,

00:05:46.062 --> 00:05:47.040
luckily I'm at a,

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:49.598
because I think I came
in with that already,

00:05:49.598 --> 00:05:50.728
and I didn't need validation

00:05:50.728 --> 00:05:51.654
from a record company-

00:05:51.654 --> 00:05:53.535
this is my third record deal.

00:05:53.535 --> 00:05:56.610
I got my first record deal when I was 19.

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:57.606
So I've seen it all,

00:05:57.606 --> 00:05:58.439
I've been through it all.

00:05:58.439 --> 00:06:00.318
The people that we've put in positions

00:06:00.318 --> 00:06:01.495
and the things we've seen

00:06:01.495 --> 00:06:03.867
and the projects that we've been behind

00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:05.750
and we've never taken credit for.

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:06.583
You know it's crazy,

00:06:06.583 --> 00:06:07.416
it'll blow your mind.

00:06:07.416 --> 00:06:08.356
When the memoirs come out,

00:06:08.356 --> 00:06:09.189
you'll be like,

00:06:09.189 --> 00:06:10.120
"oh my God".

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:11.550
But, you know,

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.068
I have a different drive.

00:06:13.068 --> 00:06:14.588
It's to create the best rap

00:06:14.588 --> 00:06:16.233
that I can possibly create.

00:06:16.233 --> 00:06:18.422
Even if no one understands it but me.

00:06:18.422 --> 00:06:20.793
It all depends on what,

00:06:20.793 --> 00:06:22.021
what makes sense.

00:06:22.021 --> 00:06:23.612
You know if the opportunity
is there to pursue it

00:06:23.612 --> 00:06:25.163
in a real big way.

00:06:25.163 --> 00:06:27.716
Some stuff I do just to do,

00:06:27.716 --> 00:06:30.440
just to get it out the way.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.367
I don't really try and feed myself into

00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:34.153
the expectations of like,

00:06:34.153 --> 00:06:35.984
other folks.

00:06:35.984 --> 00:06:37.822
'Cause they could set you up for failure,

00:06:37.822 --> 00:06:38.815
you know what I'm sayin'?

00:06:38.815 --> 00:06:40.128
Like chasing those fans

00:06:40.128 --> 00:06:42.516
"Yo we really want duduhduhduhduh".

00:06:42.516 --> 00:06:43.630
You put it on and they don't buy it.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:45.445
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:06:45.445 --> 00:06:47.488
So that'll put you in a funky spot.

00:06:47.488 --> 00:06:48.994
So it's moreso

00:06:48.994 --> 00:06:51.638
for the next kind of phase,

00:06:51.638 --> 00:06:53.257
it's just to do what works.

00:06:53.257 --> 00:06:54.932
You know what I'm saying?

00:06:54.932 --> 00:06:57.849
And we always been able to
put out stuff that works.

00:06:57.849 --> 00:06:58.682
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:06:58.682 --> 00:07:01.669
- Even in the midst of
all of the nonsense,

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:03.397
we still have a proven track record

00:07:03.397 --> 00:07:05.789
of putting out joints that work.

00:07:05.789 --> 00:07:07.177
So that's where the focus is,

00:07:07.177 --> 00:07:09.039
let's put out what works.

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.720
And if the fans dig it
and they draw to it,

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.027
cool.

00:07:12.027 --> 00:07:14.110
If they don't, cool.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:15.516
As long as it works

00:07:15.516 --> 00:07:16.349
is where it's,

00:07:16.349 --> 00:07:18.076
where we were trying to like

00:07:18.076 --> 00:07:21.019
make sure that we don't fall into a trap.

00:07:21.019 --> 00:07:23.875
Where we're kind of living
up to the expectations

00:07:23.875 --> 00:07:25.415
of people that don't support

00:07:25.415 --> 00:07:26.589
in a financial capacity.

00:07:26.589 --> 00:07:28.030
- [Interviewer] Right.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:29.100
- Because you still got to pay the bills

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:29.933
- Damn, for real.

00:07:29.933 --> 00:07:31.758
